\f1\fs22 **Halo preferred the neck\'97the carotid, just under the angle of the jaw. All it took was a practiced, upward thrust with the Point of Order, just behind the muscle where the jawline curves towards the ear.** He\'92d honed it: in one smooth motion, up and in, the three-edged spike slipping through flesh with surgical indifference, never glancing off the mandible, always finding that pulsing channel where blood pressure was highest. \
\
If he timed it for the instant of release\'97hers or his\'97the effect was sublime: **the carotid artery, pressurised and close to the surface, ruptured cleanly, erupting in a narrow, arterial geyser.** For a fraction of a second, blood would arc out in rhythm with the heart\'92s final fierce contractions, scarlet drops painting his chest, chin, hair, and whatever bore witness behind him. Anatomically, the direction mattered: angle too far back, and it missed the vessel; too far forward, and he risked catching bone or lower vein, giving him only a disappointing ooze. But when he hit it just right\'97through the platysma and into the snaking carotid\'97he achieved what he considered both artistic and forensic perfection.\
\
**From a coroner\'92s perspective, the wound was unmistakable:**\
- About a centimetre and a half below the angle of the mandible, lateral to the sternocleidomastoid.\
- Three clean edges, no fraying, with radiating blood spatter up to three metres if the victim was upright and the heart still vigorous.\
- Death, for the victim, was nearly instant\'97loss of consciousness within seconds, as the circulatory volume emptied or pressure crashed.\
\
Everything in the world for Halo, in that moment, was the beauty of the jet, the arterial arc that only the Point of Order\'97deployed at orgasm\'97could deliver.\
\
